"The letters 'Alif, Lam, Mim' at the beginning of the surah point to the miraculous nature of the Quran. These letters represent sounds originating from three basic points of articulation—the throat, the tongue, and the lips—and emphasize the literary miracle of the Quran.\n\nThe occasion of revelation of Surah ar-Rum was the period when the Byzantine (Roman) Empire suffered a great defeat against Persia. Between 613 and 616 CE, Persia had seized Syria, Palestine, Egypt, and Anatolia, bringing the Eastern Roman Empire to the brink of collapse. The Meccan polytheists rejoiced at this news and assumed an air of superiority over the Muslims. Thereupon, Allah revealed the verses announcing that the Romans would become victorious again within a few years.\n\nAllah Almighty informed that the Romans would certainly be victorious after their defeat, and that this victory would take place within a few years (between 3 and 9 years). Upon this news from our Prophet (peace be upon him), a wager was made between Abu Bakr and the polytheist Ubayy ibn Khalaf. At the indication of the Prophet (peace be upon him), Abu Bakr increased the wager to 100 camels and 9 years. Indeed, on the day of the Battle of Badr, as the Romans defeated the Persians, the Muslims also gained victory over the polytheists. Thus, this report of the unseen from the Quran was fulfilled as a miracle.\n\nAllah's promise is certain; both the victory of the Romans and the joy of the believers will come to pass. The expression 'to Allah belongs the command before and after' mentioned in the verse also indicates that following their victory, the Romans would one day be defeated by the Muslims. Indeed, in history, after defeating Persia, the Romans began to suffer defeat before the armies of Islam. This is another miracle of the Quran.\n\nThe verses state that the disbelievers only know the outward aspect of the life of this world and are heedless of the Hereafter. Yet the creation of the heavens, the earth, and man shows that everything has an end and that an account will be given in the Hereafter. Allah, reminding of the destruction of past nations, emphasizes that they wronged themselves.\n\nIn the continuation of the surah, proofs of Allah's power are enumerated: the creation of man from dust, the placement of love and mercy between spouses, the diversity of languages and colors, the alternation of night and day, sleep, seeking sustenance, and phenomena like lightning and rain all point to Allah's existence. On the Day of Resurrection, everyone will be raised and brought into His presence; while the believers enjoy blessings in Paradise, the disbelievers will suffer punishment.\n\nIn the final section of the surah, the importance of the five daily prayers is indicated: evening, night, morning, late afternoon, and noon. Glorification and praise belong exclusively to Allah. He brings out the living from the dead and the dead from the living, and gives life to the dead earth. In the same way, human beings will be brought forth from their graves.\n\nOne of the verses pointing to Allah's existence and power is His creation of man from dust. Had nature operated on its own, an advanced being like man could not have emerged from inanimate dust. Likewise, His creating spouses from among their own kind and placing love and mercy between them are among the proofs of His mercy. This order shows that humanity is prepared for high morality and civilization, and that woman must be protected.\n\nThe creation of the heavens and the earth, and the diversity of languages and colors are also among Allah's signs. This diversity demonstrates His power and knowledge. Moreover, different nations and tribes were created so that people may know one another. Differences in language and color hold significant lessons for people of knowledge; knowing multiple languages serves as a means to better understand Allah's signs.\n\nSleeping during the night and day and seeking sustenance are also among Allah's blessings. Sleep is rest, while work is seeking His bounty. While working, one must recognize that earnings come from Allah, seek progress every day, and hope for His grace. These verses contain lessons for communities that stay alert and listen.\n\nAllah shows lightning to cause fear and hope, and sends down water from the sky to revive the dead earth. This is proof of His power to resurrect the dead. Furthermore, the heaven and the earth stand by His command; when He summons you, you will emerge from the earth. Whatever is in the heavens and the earth belongs to Him; all are obedient to Him.\n\nIt is Allah Who originates creation and then repeats it; and repeating it is even easier for Him. To Him belong the highest attributes; He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise. Allah presents to you an example from yourselves: Would any of your slaves ever be your partners? Associating partners with Allah is just as baseless. The wrongdoers follow their desires without knowledge, and no one can guide the one whom Allah has led astray.\n\nO Prophet! Set your face toward the religion as a hanif (an upright monotheist), toward the innate disposition (fitrah) upon which Allah has created mankind. There is no altering Allah's creation. That is the correct religion, but most people do not know. Fitrah is the primordial nature upon which every child is born; later, their parents make them Jewish or Christian. Human nature is inherently inclined to recognize Allah and worship Him.\n\nWhen an affliction touches people, they cry out to their Lord, but once they taste mercy from Him, a group of them immediately associate partners with Him. They show ingratitude for the blessings granted to them. We have not sent down to them any authority justifying what they associate with Him. When We cause people to taste mercy, they rejoice in it, but if an evil befalls them, they fall into despair. Allah expands sustenance for whom He wills and restricts it. So give the relative, the poor, and the traveler their due; this is best for those who desire Allah's pleasure.\n\nUsury (riba) does not increase with Allah; but zakah increases manifold. It is Allah Who created you, then provided for you, then will cause you to die, and then will bring you to life; can any of your associated partners do any of these things? Exalted is Allah far above what they associate with Him.\n\nReligion rests on two sources: fitrah (innate nature) and acquisition. Fitrah is a divine inclination that Allah has placed in our creation, directing us toward Him. Acquisition, however, since it is shaped by our feelings and thoughts, can lead to desires contrary to fitrah. For this reason, Allah commands us to turn toward the religion as a hanif (one who affirms the oneness of Allah) and to cling to fitrah. We must not attempt to alter Allah's creation, for corrupting the fitrah cannot restore the capacity we have lost.\n\nIn verse 31, everyone is commanded to turn to Allah in repentance and sincerity. In verse 32, the polytheists who divided their religion and became sects are condemned. They abandoned the fitrah and, driven by narrow-mindedness, followed their desires and split into factions. Yet the true religion consists of uprightly following the fitrah upon which all human beings were created. However, most people do not know this and follow customs or their own desires.\n\nWhen people fall into hardship, by their very nature they implore Allah alone. But once Allah causes them to taste His mercy, some of them associate partners with Him. For instance, our nation pleaded in this manner during the Battles of Çanakkale. Verses 36–37 describe those who become arrogant when blessing arrives, yet fall into despair in hardship. In the noble hadith, the believer is likened to a fresh crop; as the wind blows, it bends and rises again. The disbeliever, on the other hand, is like a pine tree: once knocked down, it cannot rise again. Allah extends sustenance to whom He wills and restricts it.\n\nIn verse 38, the practical aspect of religion is summarized: giving relatives, the poor, and travelers their due. Those who seek Allah's pleasure will attain salvation. In verse 39, the subject of usury is addressed. Some state that conventional usury is meant here, while others say it refers to gifts given with the expectation of receiving more in return. In any case, usury and similar transactions do not increase with Allah; true increase lies in zakah and charity. In verse 40, Allah's attributes of creating, sustaining, causing to die, and bringing to life are recalled, emphasizing that the associated partners can do none of these things.\n\nIn verses 41–53, it is stated that corruption has appeared on land and sea because of the sins people have committed. Allah makes them taste some of the punishment in this world so that they may turn in repentance. They are commanded to travel through the earth to take a lesson from the end of past nations. The duty of the Prophet (peace be upon him) is to warn; however, he cannot make the disbelievers, who are like the dead, or the deaf hear. The winds, rain, and the revival of plants are signs of Allah's power. Likewise, He is capable of raising the dead.\n\nIn verses 54–60, it is recalled that man is created in a state of weakness, then gains strength, and then becomes weak again. On the Day of Resurrection, the sinners swear that they remained in the world for only a short while. The believers, however, tell them that the true duration extends until the Day of Resurrection. On that day, excuses will be of no avail. The Quran has set forth every kind of parable; yet the disbelievers still consider it a lie. Allah seals the hearts of those who do not know. The Prophet is commanded to be patient and to trust in Allah's promise, lest those who lack firm faith sway him toward laxity."